    "config_sha256": "b7b51aa97e7c7ff52c7de4ef8ce0c93bab47a1bc6cdcc68452b668e146f9d647",
    "config_sha256_source": "git blob of benchmarks/compare/gigaxml-config.yaml. Hashed over the blob rather than the working tree because core.autocrlf=true makes a fresh clone 524 bytes of CRLF where the blob is 512 bytes of LF, and a working-tree digest would be true only on the machine that took it. The config has exactly one commit in its history (51609be) and is byte-identical at 82e31eb and at HEAD, so this digest does describe the config the sweep read.",
    "dataset_sha256": "NOT RECORDED, and unrecoverable. run_comparison.py requires data/b100m.xml, b1g.xml and b4g.xml to exist and skips any that do not; it never hashed them, never recorded the generate command or its seed, and all three have since been deleted. gigaxml generate is a pure function of (seed, size, namespace) and is byte-reproducible -- measured, two runs at the same seed are identical -- so the bytes are recoverable in principle, but the SEED THAT PRODUCED THESE FILES was never written down, and an unrecorded seed cannot be guessed from a digest. This is the one field the milestone's invariant cannot supply for this file, and it is why benchmarks/provenance.py now records seed and digest together.",
    "memory_method": "peak_wset (self-read in child) -- as recorded, and it was TRUE for this sweep, which is decidable rather than assumed: results.json records os 'Windows 11' and on win32 psutil's memory_info() has peak_wset, so the wrapper took the peak branch and not its 'or info.rss' fallback. It was still a hardcoded string at the time, so nothing in the code guaranteed it; run_comparison.py now has the child report which counter answered and the recorder store that instead of asserting it.",
    "summary_recomputable": "yes, from this file's own runs[]: benchmarks/provenance.py recompute_summary rebuilds all 12 data points and all 12 agree. The one field that is NOT derivable from runs[] alone is complete_output, which compares rows_written against rows_in_document -- an expected count that is an input to the check, not a measurement. It is load-bearing: it is what caught 4GB/pandas writing 10,485,760 of 11,915,264 rows and exiting 0.",
    "measurement_code_note": "run_comparison.py has changed in three committed ways since 82e31eb (e837d7c, 2ef00be): the version reader, a count_data_rows edge-case fix, and ruff formatting. None alters what was measured. The count_data_rows fix was checked rather than assumed -- old and new agree on all six inputs tried, including the empty file and the one-byte file the fix was written for -- so the recorded row counts stand."
  },
